    "why": "THE SATURATED POINT THAT IS NOT IN OVERLOAD. The study's throughput claim currently rests on three tiers and none of them is quotable for capacity planning: T20 at 99.6% saturation returns +0.06% because there is no queue, T14 at 86.3% returns +8.6%, and T21 at 55.9% returns +20.8% but sits in OVERLOAD -- 30-39 s mean latency, TTFT p99 of 34-45 s, and neither arm finished the trace (66.1% and 76.9% of 9,621 requests). No SLO is meetable there, so no operator would ever run in that regime and the number cannot be sold. 28 req/s is the geometric mean of T15's 20 (99.5%, unsaturated) and T21's 40 (55.9%, overloaded), so it brackets the knee from inside. PREDICTION registered before the run: saturation lands between 70% and 88% and MPS returns between +8% and +16%, with mean latency still in single-digit seconds. If latency is again in the tens of seconds the knee is below 28 and the tier should be re-run at 24. Identical tenants, so per section 0 there is no deep/shallow split to report and the aggregate figure applies to both -- which is exactly what makes it quotable."
